1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,059
Here's how to reverse engineer Zylo from a written spec.

2
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:06,600
Claude passed all thirty-one checks, and found six gaps in my spec.

3
00:00:07,417 --> 00:00:09,188
We gave Claude one Markdown file.

4
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:12,495
It copies the part of Zylo a smaller company actually needs.

5
00:00:12,836 --> 00:00:16,975
One shared list of every paid tool, each with an owner and a department.

6
00:00:17,273 --> 00:00:20,337
The yearly cost, the renewal date and the notice period.

7
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,207
Seats bought and seats used.

8
00:00:22,420 --> 00:00:25,361
And a reminder thirty days before the last day to cancel.

9
00:00:26,450 --> 00:00:28,327
The core rule is the notice deadline.

10
00:00:28,583 --> 00:00:32,850
A contract renews on its renewal date, but you have to cancel before its notice period starts.

11
00:00:33,234 --> 00:00:37,985
Zoom renews on December first with thirty days' notice, so the last day to cancel is November first.

12
00:00:38,213 --> 00:00:43,635
The owner's reminder comes thirty days before that deadline, not before the renewal, because by then it's often too late.

13
00:00:44,717 --> 00:00:47,319
Claude built it in four phases, one prompt each.

14
00:00:47,575 --> 00:00:50,263
The foundation, with people, departments and settings.

15
00:00:50,519 --> 00:00:53,101
The inventory, the money rules and department totals.

16
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:55,682
Renewals, status changes and reminders.

17
00:00:55,981 --> 00:00:58,022
Then CSV import and export.

18
00:00:58,187 --> 00:01:02,622
After every phase, the acceptance checks ran in a real browser, from fresh seed data.

19
00:01:03,683 --> 00:01:04,579
Here's what it built.

20
00:01:04,878 --> 00:01:11,000
The inventory shows a thirty-four thousand one hundred and twenty dollar annual run rate, and the estimated cost of unused seats.

21
00:01:11,320 --> 00:01:15,350
GitHub's page shows its cost, its renewal, and a history of every change.

22
00:01:15,582 --> 00:01:19,827
Renewals groups tools by notice deadline, with four to decide within thirty days.

23
00:01:20,147 --> 00:01:24,585
The CSV import previews every row, and stays locked until the errors are fixed.

24
00:01:24,883 --> 00:01:27,424
And on a phone, the tiles fit in two columns.

25
00:01:28,517 --> 00:01:34,042
In the final run, on a fresh database, all thirty-one checks passed, along with ninety-nine automated tests.

26
00:01:34,234 --> 00:01:36,711
A second full browser run passed all thirty-one too.

27
00:01:37,817 --> 00:01:39,823
But the more useful result is what broke.

28
00:01:40,079 --> 00:01:42,489
The build exposed six gaps in the spec.

29
00:01:42,724 --> 00:01:44,388
Places where Claude had to guess.

30
00:01:44,601 --> 00:01:47,520
Admins could deactivate a person, but never bring them back.

31
00:01:47,707 --> 00:01:50,993
Restoring a cancelled tool could give it an owner who had already left.

32
00:01:51,505 --> 00:01:54,043
And a late cancellation had no place in the history.

33
00:01:54,406 --> 00:01:56,272
Every gap is now fixed in the spec.

34
00:01:57,383 --> 00:01:59,858
Passing every check isn't the same as production-ready.

35
00:02:00,114 --> 00:02:05,703
We didn't test production sign-in, real email delivery, the daily job on a real scheduler, or hosting.

36
00:02:05,916 --> 00:02:08,221
Those are yours to check before your company relies on it.

37
00:02:09,317 --> 00:02:10,661
So is it worth building?

38
00:02:11,109 --> 00:02:12,431
Zylo doesn't publish prices.

39
00:02:12,687 --> 00:02:16,038
Its Core, Premium and Enterprise plans are all sold by quote.

40
00:02:16,231 --> 00:02:22,268
If you have hundreds of tools and need them found in your accounting data, with seat usage pulled in automatically, buy Zylo.

41
00:02:22,588 --> 00:02:27,013
If your tools fit in a spreadsheet, and renewals keep slipping past the notice deadline, build it.

42
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:29,406
Everything is in one file.

43
00:02:29,662 --> 00:02:36,537
The data model, nineteen rules, seed data, four phase prompts, and all thirty-one checks, with the six fixes already in.

44
00:02:37,533 --> 00:02:40,975
The full spec is free at personal software factory dot com.

45
00:02:40,975 --> 00:02:43,075
If you build it, tell me what broke.

46
00:02:43,075 --> 00:02:43,542
I'm Sam.

47
00:02:43,542 --> 00:02:44,533
See you tomorrow.
